// CARD a5-01 · SEE: two real portraits as prints, Kjeld in front; on "stepped" his print greys, a tag 1979–2004 · 25 YEARS ties on and it slides back; on "warning" Knudstorp's print slides forward into the light · SEAM in right
# cine act=after fx="code:@baton_portraits;a:lego_kjeld_1996_award;b:lego_knudstorp_2010;alab:KJELD KIRK KRISTIANSEN;atag:CEO 1979–2004;blab:JØRGEN VIG KNUDSTORP;go:stepped;go2:new;in:right"
{tense} In October two thousand and four, Kjeld stepped back after twenty-five years as chief **executive**.
The new chief executive was the man who had written the **warning**.

// CARD a5-02 · SEE: Knudstorp speaking, the camera travelling in toward his face; on "Knudstorp" his name card slides in at the top · SEAM out right
# cine act=after fx="code:@name_strap;pic:lego_knudstorp_2010;go:Knudstorp;name:JØRGEN VIG KNUDSTORP · 35;role:CEO from October 2004 · first outsider in a generation;fx:.5;fy:-1;out:right"
Knudstorp was thirty-five, and the first chief executive from outside the family in a **generation**.

// CARD a5-03 · SEE: hands feeding notes through a counting machine, dim; a black LED board over it; on "cash" CASH clicks up in amber; then his verbatim words light in amber dots as the quote voice reads them · SEAM in right · HOLD
# cine act=after fx="code:@led_quote;bg:stock_money_counting_machine_03;head:CASH;go:cash;q:“At first I actually said, let's not talk about strategy, let's talk about an action plan, to address the debt, to get the cash flow.”;span:6;by:JØRGEN VIG KNUDSTORP · 2011 interview, via Bloomberg · read by a voice actor;in:right"
He said his first job wasn't strategy. It was **cash**.
@knudstorp: At first I actually said, let's not talk about strategy, let's talk about an action plan, to address the debt, to get the cash flow.

// CARD a5-04 · SEE: two prints across a dark gap: Ole Kirk's house (KIRKBI · THE FAMILY) and a LEGO factory; on "lent" a bridge of yellow bricks builds across the gap while DKK 800M counts up over it · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@bridge_loan;a:lego_ole_kirks_house_01;b:lego_factory_kladno;alab:KIRKBI · THE FAMILY;blab:LEGO;go:lent;val:800;pre:DKK;unit:M;lab:LENT TO LEGO · 2004;out:right"
The family's holding company, KIRKBI, lent LEGO eight hundred million **kroner**.

// CARD a5-05 · SEE: a pen signing a contract, dim; a red tag on a string reads GIFT; on "loan" it spins round: LOAN · CIBOR + 3% · DUE DECEMBER 2011 · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@gift_tag_flip;clip:stock_finance_signing_contract_10;front:GIFT;back:LOAN;terms:CIBOR + 3%~DUE DECEMBER 2011~LOCKED WHILE EQUITY < 40%;go:loan;in:right"
{wry} Not a gift. A loan, with interest, due in twenty **eleven**. (J)

// CARD a5-06 · SEE: an empty factory line (AI moment art) as a big print; on "fifth" it tears and its last fifth falls out of frame; −20% sets in the gap with DKK 1,523M and ABOUT 1,000 JOBS · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@tear_fifth;pic:lego_art_factory_line_empty;go:fifth;frac:.2;lab:−20%;sub:DKK 1,523M~ABOUT 1,000 JOBS;out:right"
Costs were cut by a fifth, more than one and a half billion kroner, and about a thousand jobs **went**.

// CARD a5-07 · SEE: a heap of bricks fills the frame under a counter reading 12,000+; on "cut" a sweep clears most of them out of frame and the counter runs down to 7,000 · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@pile_sweep;bg:lego_brick_pile_mixed;go:cut;from:12000;to:7000;n:150;keep:.45;lab:DIFFERENT PARTS;in:right"
The parts library was cut back to about seven **thousand**.

// CARD a5-08 · SEE: a sorting machine working behind; a wall of colour chips, one per shade; on "half" every other chip drops out and falls · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@colour_halve;clip:lego_clip_factory_sorting;n:100;go:half;lab:ABOUT 100 COLOURS → ABOUT 50;out:right"
So the colours were cut roughly in **half**.

// CARD a5-09 · MOTIF · SEE: real Technic gears meshed together; the brick drops and lands in a gap among them; EVERY PART EARNS ITS PLACE sets above · SEAM in right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@brick_drop;bg:lego_technic_gears;go:earn;x:.5;y:.58;h:.1;lab:EVERY PART EARNS ITS PLACE;lx:.5;z:.25;move:right;in:right"
And from then on, a new part had to earn its **place**.

// CARD a5-10 · SEE: the real log-flume ride at LEGOLAND Billund, the camera travelling along it · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@reel;under:lego_legoland_logflume;ua:0;move:right;z:.4;out:right"
Then the parks went **too**.

// CARD a5-11 · SEE: the camera travels across LEGOLAND's real castle; on "sold" a red SOLD strip sweeps across its top corner; €375M · JULY 2005 · CONTROL TO BLACKSTONE sets under · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@corner_banner;pic:lego_legoland_castle;word:SOLD;terms:€375M · JULY 2005 · CONTROL TO BLACKSTONE;go:sold;in:right"
In July two thousand and five, LEGO sold control of LEGOLAND for three hundred and seventy-five million **euros**.

// CARD a5-12 · SEE: the real Miniland harbour at LEGOLAND; a ring of shares is drawn round it; on "kept" the 70% sold swings away, LEGO 15% and KIRKBI 15% stay lit · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@pie_on_photo;pic:lego_legoland_modern;slices:BLACKSTONE/MERLIN^70^0^#6d655b~LEGO^15^1^#f2c14e~KIRKBI^15^1^#e08a3a;go:kept;out:right"
LEGO and its family holding company kept thirty **percent**.

// CARD a5-13 · SEE: a boy building a tower of bricks, dim; one bar hangs below a zero line in red, −1,931 (2004); on "profit" it swings up through zero to +505 in gold (2005) · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@swing_bar;bg:lego_clip_blocks_boy;a:-1931;b:505;go:profit;alab:2004 · NET;blab:2005 · NET;unit:M DKK;in:right"
{warm} In two thousand and five, LEGO made a profit again: five hundred and five million kroner, **net**.

// CARD a5-14 · SEE: a film strip of real frames slides across the dark, each something LEGO let go: the parks, own worlds like Galidor, half the colours, 5,000 parts, about 1,000 jobs; a red line strikes each as it passes · SEAM out right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@filmstrip_recap;frames:lego_legoland_logflume^PARKS~lego_doc_wayback_home_2002_crop^OWN WORLDS LIKE GALIDOR~lego_clip_factory_sorting^HALF THE COLOURS~lego_brick_pile_mixed^5,000 PARTS~lego_art_factory_line_empty^ABOUT 1,000 JOBS;go:Sales;lab:SALES 2005 · ABOUT +5%;sub:DKK 6,704M → 7,050M · LEGO annual reports;out:right"
Sales grew only a little. The swing came from what LEGO stopped **doing**.
